<section class="band band-tint" aria-labelledby="what-heading">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl">
    <h2 id="what-heading" class="section-title text-center">What is OpenTaint?</h2>
    <p class="subheadline">
      The open source taint analysis engine for the AI era. OpenTaint is a formal dataflow analysis tool you can customize and self-host. AI agents drive the application security analysis, while the engine reuses what they learn without burning tokens on every scan. It is an AI-ready open source alternative to Semgrep Pro and CodeQL.
    </p>

    <h2 id="security-debt-heading" class="section-title mt-12 lg:mt-14 text-center">Application security is the new tech debt.</h2>
    <p class="subheadline">
      AI makes code cheaper to produce and harder to fully review. Security work moves downstream into review queues, remediation backlogs, and incident response, while attackers automate discovery too. Application security becomes tech debt an attacker can force you to repay. You start paying for that debt long before an incident.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-left lg:mt-10 lg:space-y-5" aria-labelledby="security-debt-heading">
      {pressures.map((pressure) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3 font-mono text-[15px] leading-[185%] lg:text-base lg:gap-4">
          <span class="mt-[0.65em] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div>
            <strong class="feature-title">{pressure.title}</strong>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">{pressure.description}</span>
            <span class="mt-2 block font-medium text-foreground">{pressure.value}</span>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-heading" class="section-title mt-12 lg:mt-14 text-center">How OpenTaint changes the economics</h2>
    <p class="subheadline">
      OpenTaint breaks that cycle by separating learning from searching. Learning application context is expensive and unpredictable. Once the agent turns that context into rules and summaries, the engine can search for known dataflow patterns cheaply and deterministically.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-left lg:mt-10 lg:space-y-5" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
      {features.map((feature) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3 font-mono text-[15px] leading-[185%] lg:text-base lg:gap-4">
          <span class="mt-[0.65em] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div>
            <strong class="feature-title">{feature.title}</strong>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">{feature.description}</span>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 id="proof-heading" class="section-title mt-12 lg:mt-14 text-center">See what the engine catches</h2>
    <p class="subheadline">Claims about detection depth need traces and results you can inspect.</p>

    <ul class="mt-8 space-y-4 text-left lg:mt-10 lg:space-y-5" aria-labelledby="proof-heading">
      {proofs.map((proof) => (
        <li class="flex gap-3 font-mono text-[15px] leading-[185%] lg:text-base lg:gap-4">
          <span class="mt-[0.65em] h-1.5 w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-primary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <div>
            <a href={proof.href} class="feature-title underline decoration-primary/40 underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-primary">{proof.title}</a>
            <span class="text-muted-foreground">{proof.description}</span>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </div>

</section>
